WEBVTT

00:01.373 --> 00:04.681
♪ It seems today that all you see ♪

00:04.682 --> 00:08.121
♪ Is violence in movies and sex on TV ♪

00:08.156 --> 00:11.691
♪ But where are those
good old-fashioned values ♪

00:11.726 --> 00:14.694
♪ On which we used to rely? ♪

00:14.729 --> 00:18.197
♪ Lucky there's a family guy ♪

00:18.233 --> 00:21.501
♪ Lucky there's a man who
positively can do ♪

00:21.536 --> 00:23.069
♪ All the things that make us ♪

00:23.104 --> 00:24.437
♪ Laugh and cry ♪

00:24.472 --> 00:30.043
♪ He's... a... Fam... ily... Guy! ♪

00:31.323 --> 00:32.779
Hi, I'm Peter Griffin,

00:32.814 --> 00:35.314
and I'm here at
The Museum of Television & Radio

00:35.350 --> 00:37.083
<i>to commemorate Family Guy</i>

00:37.118 --> 00:40.153
becoming the longest-running
show in television history.

00:40.188 --> 00:45.625
<i>For 60 years, Sunday has meant
God, football and Family Guy.</i>

00:45.660 --> 00:48.327
<i>And later, to a lesser degree,
The Simpsons.</i>

00:48.363 --> 00:51.230
Premiering in 1952 as a recurring sketch

00:51.266 --> 00:53.633
<i>in the popular Dow Chemical Follies,</i>

00:53.668 --> 00:56.469
<i>Family Guy has survived
19 cancellations,</i>

00:56.504 --> 00:58.104
two assassination attempts,

00:58.139 --> 01:00.740
<i>and a pretty good ribbing
by those South Park guys.</i>

01:00.775 --> 01:03.443
Well, tonight we take
a look back at classic episodes

01:03.478 --> 01:05.511
from our first three decades.

01:05.547 --> 01:07.180
Uhp, here come some tourists.

01:07.215 --> 01:10.983
I know, I'll sit here
and pretend I'm a wax figure.

01:11.019 --> 01:12.618
Who is that?

01:12.654 --> 01:15.054
<i>That's the fat idiot from
The Family Guy.</i>

01:15.090 --> 01:17.857
- Ugh. I hate that show.
- Me, too.

01:24.432 --> 01:26.332
<i>Raleigh Kids Cigarettes</i>

01:26.367 --> 01:28.768
<i>is happy to bring you Family Guy,</i>

01:28.803 --> 01:32.238
<i>the story of an American family
named the Griffins.</i>

01:32.273 --> 01:34.107
<i>This is Peter,</i>

01:34.142 --> 01:36.042
<i>breadwinner and head of household.</i>

01:36.077 --> 01:38.578
<i>Lois is the name of his wife.</i>

01:38.613 --> 01:40.313
<i>These are their children.</i>

01:40.348 --> 01:43.015
<i>This is Chris, the firstborn son.</i>

01:43.051 --> 01:46.586
<i>This is little Stewie.
Oh, boy, he's a handful.</i>

01:46.621 --> 01:48.087
<i>Meg.</i>

01:48.123 --> 01:51.324
<i>And introducing Doodles Weaver
as Brian the Dog.</i>

01:53.328 --> 01:55.962
We don't trust just any
cigarette for our children.

01:55.997 --> 01:59.232
Raleigh Kids is the only
cigarette made for tiny hands.

01:59.267 --> 02:02.235
It's why it's the playground
favorite. Right, kids?

02:04.939 --> 02:06.339
<i>And now, Raleigh Kids,</i>

02:06.374 --> 02:08.508
<i>also the makers of Kinder Coffee,</i>

02:08.543 --> 02:11.244
<i>invites you to watch Family Guy.</i>

02:12.480 --> 02:15.781
Dad, I overheard the fellas talking.

02:15.817 --> 02:17.817
Can I ask you a question about girls?

02:17.852 --> 02:19.785
Of course, son. You're a young man now.

02:19.821 --> 02:21.254
It's natural to be curious.

02:21.289 --> 02:22.655
Thanks, Pop.

02:22.690 --> 02:24.624
What's "knuckle-dunk"?

02:24.659 --> 02:26.225
Uhp, atomic test.

02:26.261 --> 02:27.760
Put on your glasses.

02:27.795 --> 02:29.562
Never mind what your friends said.

02:29.597 --> 02:32.298
If you want to have fun
with your girl, try dancing.

02:32.333 --> 02:34.200
It's fun and good for your health.

02:36.237 --> 02:37.937
Swell, that's helpful.

02:37.972 --> 02:39.839
So, what's going on if a guy says

02:39.874 --> 02:42.842
a girl "has a red scare
in her Harry Truman"?

02:42.877 --> 02:44.877
Fence.

02:50.418 --> 02:52.418
Oh, look, here comes the milkman.

02:55.857 --> 02:57.924
So convenient.

02:57.959 --> 02:59.192
Good evening, everyone.

02:59.227 --> 03:02.094
I'm Tom Tucker,
and this is the Radio 5 news.

03:02.130 --> 03:04.964
Our top story this evening,
songsmith Elvis Presley

03:04.999 --> 03:07.633
<i>will be appearing on The Ed
Sullivan Show this Sunday night</i>

03:07.669 --> 03:10.503
on that never-gonna-last fad
called television.

03:10.538 --> 03:14.106
And now a word from our sponsor:
Fred Trump Apartments.

03:14.142 --> 03:17.243
If you don't want to live
with blacks, Fred Trump.

03:17.278 --> 03:21.180
Wow, Elvis Presley!
Can we get a television set?

03:21.216 --> 03:22.915
Sorry, Chris, we don't got the money.

03:22.951 --> 03:24.417
I spent our entire savings

03:24.452 --> 03:27.053
building that stupid fallout
shelter we never get to use.

03:28.423 --> 03:30.289
Why don't you just buy a TV
for the weekend,

03:30.325 --> 03:31.657
watch Elvis,

03:31.693 --> 03:33.626
and then return it on Monday
and say it doesn't work?

03:33.661 --> 03:35.628
Boy, even in the '50s, you're a scumbag.

03:35.663 --> 03:38.264
- That's a great idea, boy!
- Hang on.

03:38.299 --> 03:41.100
Why don't I just get a job
to help pay for it?

03:41.135 --> 03:43.469
What? No wife of mine is working.

03:43.504 --> 03:45.538
There's underpants in this house
that need starching.

03:45.573 --> 03:47.773
I don't want to go to work
in soft underpants

03:47.809 --> 03:50.810
like some Nancy boy;
I want to go to work like this.

03:53.548 --> 03:56.315
Stiff as a plate. That's underpants.

04:01.356 --> 04:04.423
We got some time before Elvis.
Can we see what else is on?

04:04.459 --> 04:05.992
Sure thing.

04:06.027 --> 04:09.495
We now return to 1950s sci-fi,

04:09.530 --> 04:12.798
which is always a man in a
silver suit battling a monster.

04:12.834 --> 04:14.533
Leave us humans alone.

04:14.569 --> 04:18.271
It's 1994,
and the moon belongs to Earth.

04:21.509 --> 04:23.009
Rawr.

04:29.984 --> 04:31.884
It's safe now.

04:31.919 --> 04:34.587
How's the soil, Professor Knockers?

04:34.622 --> 04:35.788
It's good.

04:35.823 --> 04:39.025
Good. Good.

04:39.060 --> 04:42.795
Good evening. I'm Tom Tucker,
previously of Radio 5 news.

04:42.830 --> 04:44.897
Tonight, I bring you a special report.

04:44.932 --> 04:47.933
Women in the workplace:
hilarious or disturbing?

04:52.006 --> 04:55.341
I am a 1950s man

04:55.376 --> 04:57.543
speaking in a stilted manner,

04:57.578 --> 05:01.280
seemingly with
no self-awareness whatsoever.

05:01.316 --> 05:05.184
I am shouting for some reason

05:05.219 --> 05:10.189
and will now suddenly be
replaced by crude graphics.

05:14.896 --> 05:18.864
This factory has been beset by women.

05:18.900 --> 05:21.200
Is your factory next?

05:25.573 --> 05:28.240
Lois, that's you! You took a job?

05:28.276 --> 05:30.176
Sorry, Peter. I wanted to tell you,

05:30.211 --> 05:32.111
but you were so dead set against it.

05:32.146 --> 05:33.512
Damn right I'm against it!

05:33.548 --> 05:35.548
If God wanted women in the workplace,

05:35.583 --> 05:37.116
he'd have made them alcoholics.

05:37.151 --> 05:38.851
Now, you're quitting that job tomorrow.

05:38.886 --> 05:41.620
- No, I'm not.
- Quiet. Elvis is on.

05:41.656 --> 05:44.590
Ladies and gentlemen, Elvis Presley.

05:46.961 --> 05:49.929
- Huh?
- What?! Elvis Presley is white?

05:49.964 --> 05:51.597
Cleveland, did you know about this?

05:51.632 --> 05:54.567
Come on, Donna, let's pick up
some gizzards and fried skins

05:54.602 --> 05:56.502
and head on down to the juke joint.

05:56.537 --> 05:58.804
Tonight it's gonna be jumpin'!

06:00.608 --> 06:03.743
<i>And it was jumpin'. It really was.</i>

06:03.778 --> 06:05.578
<i>But I was banished to the sidewalk</i>

06:05.613 --> 06:09.448
<i>because I called one of
the band guys a bad name.</i>

06:11.986 --> 06:15.321
I thought you were bringing the TV back.

06:15.356 --> 06:17.623
I changed my mind. This TV is awesome!

06:17.658 --> 06:19.291
I've been watching it all day.

06:19.327 --> 06:21.727
You can keep your job
so we can afford this thing.

06:21.763 --> 06:25.030
- Oh, that's great news, Peter.
- You know, Lois, I had an idea.

06:25.066 --> 06:27.867
Instead of eating
in another room and talking,

06:27.902 --> 06:31.203
what if we bring the food
out here and never talk again?

06:31.239 --> 06:34.306
- That's a great idea.
- Shh.

06:38.646 --> 06:41.280
Are you looking for
the perfect breakfast meal?

06:41.315 --> 06:44.250
Try Post Raisin Bran, made with raisins,

06:44.285 --> 06:46.619
which are grapes
that have been dried in the sun

06:46.654 --> 06:48.587
for a long period of time;

06:48.623 --> 06:52.491
and bran, the hard outer layers
of cereal grain.

06:52.527 --> 06:56.796
Along with germ, it is an
integral part of whole grains.

06:56.831 --> 06:59.432
When you eat Raisin Bran,
it fills your stomach

06:59.467 --> 07:02.168
and keeps you from feeling
the sensation of hunger.

07:02.203 --> 07:04.336
"Hunger" being pangs in the belly

07:04.372 --> 07:06.572
as a result of lack of nourishment,

07:06.607 --> 07:09.375
thereby sending signals
to your brain telling you,

07:09.410 --> 07:11.143
"Hey, I'm hungry."

07:11.179 --> 07:13.979
And when you hear Mr. "Hey, I'm Hungry,"

07:14.015 --> 07:17.416
be sure to have a hearty bowl
of Post Raisin Bran.

07:17.452 --> 07:20.419
Again, made with raisins,
which are grapes

07:20.455 --> 07:23.422
that have been dried in the sun
for a long period of time;

07:23.458 --> 07:26.992
and bran, the hard outer layers
of cereal grain.

07:27.028 --> 07:30.996
Along with germ, it is an
integral part of whole grains.

07:33.000 --> 07:36.368
♪ Post Raisin Bran ♪

07:36.404 --> 07:39.305
♪ Made with raisins, which are
grapes that have been dried ♪

07:39.340 --> 07:44.410
♪ In the sun for a long period of time ♪

07:44.445 --> 07:46.712
♪ And bran ♪

07:46.747 --> 07:50.683
♪ The hard outer layers
of cereal grain ♪

07:50.718 --> 07:52.418
♪ Along with germ ♪

07:52.453 --> 07:56.856
♪ It is an integral part
of whole grains ♪

07:56.891 --> 08:00.693
♪ Post Raisin Bran. ♪

08:00.728 --> 08:02.995
Boy, these commercials go by fast.

08:03.030 --> 08:05.231
You can barely absorb any of it.

08:05.266 --> 08:07.199
I don't know how you do it, Peter.

08:07.235 --> 08:08.334
Can we get some Raisin Bran?

08:08.369 --> 08:10.035
Do we have any Raisin Bran in the house?

08:10.071 --> 08:13.706
Just working day after day, I'm
exhausted, and I miss my family.

08:13.741 --> 08:15.307
It's made with raisins, which are grapes

08:15.343 --> 08:18.143
that have been dried in the sun
for a long period of time.

08:18.179 --> 08:20.346
I gave my notice,
and tomorrow's my last day.

08:20.381 --> 08:22.681
So I guess we have to get rid of the TV.

08:22.717 --> 08:24.183
By the way, Chris and Raisin Bran

08:24.218 --> 08:26.252
are at Raisin Bran practice.

08:29.223 --> 08:32.424
- Peter, I got a surprise for you.
- Raisin Bran?

08:32.460 --> 08:33.926
A television?

08:33.961 --> 08:35.895
We can't afford this; you quit your job.

08:35.930 --> 08:38.030
This is what we were making on the line.

08:38.065 --> 08:41.333
The only reason I wanted to work
was to make this for you.

08:41.369 --> 08:44.737
Oh, Lois, you're the greatest.

08:50.244 --> 08:54.213
I guess this is why
women shouldn't work.

08:54.248 --> 08:56.382
To the moon, Lois!

08:56.417 --> 08:58.651
"To the moon"? Wh-What does that mean?

08:58.686 --> 09:00.519
You know, "to-to the moon."

09:00.555 --> 09:02.555
Okay, you're threatening
to punch me so hard

09:02.590 --> 09:04.189
I'm gonna fly to the moon?

09:04.225 --> 09:07.326
Like-like it's funny
to hit me so violently

09:07.361 --> 09:10.162
my body will fly out of the atmosphere?

09:12.166 --> 09:14.166
Well, it's not funny anymore.

09:21.843 --> 09:24.711
♪ Hi, I'm Peter ♪

09:24.746 --> 09:26.946
♪ This is Lois ♪

09:26.981 --> 09:30.383
♪ My sons Stewie and Chris ♪

09:30.418 --> 09:33.519
♪ Our dog Brian, and the rest is this ♪

09:35.523 --> 09:38.791
♪ We live in Quahog, near the beach ♪

09:38.827 --> 09:42.361
♪ A wizard gave my baby
the power of speech ♪

09:42.397 --> 09:45.732
♪ No one can hear him,
but when he's away ♪

09:45.767 --> 09:48.935
♪ People in town can hear what he says ♪

09:50.939 --> 09:53.439
<i>♪ This is Family Guy. ♪</i>

09:53.475 --> 09:55.842
The dog talks, too!

09:59.914 --> 10:01.347
Good morning, family.

10:01.382 --> 10:04.117
I'll have my regular breakfast
and the newspaper, please.

10:04.152 --> 10:06.786
Okay, here's your highball,
a grapefruit,

10:06.821 --> 10:08.955
and our one weird serrated spoon.

10:08.990 --> 10:12.859
- And Raisin Bran?
- Yes.

10:12.894 --> 10:15.928
But sorry, there's no newspaper.
Chris quit his paper route.

10:15.964 --> 10:18.564
- What?
- Aw, jobs are for bozos.

10:18.600 --> 10:20.466
Besides, I won't be around to work.

10:20.502 --> 10:23.569
Me and my pals are going to
Woodstock to smoke dope.

10:23.605 --> 10:27.373
No, you are not!
Chris Griffin, you are grounded.

10:27.408 --> 10:28.975
Oh, I hate you!

10:29.010 --> 10:31.778
Now I'll never get to see Sha Na Na!

10:35.016 --> 10:38.117
Hello. I just moved in down the block.

10:38.153 --> 10:41.220
My name's Herbert, not Roy Mitchell.

10:41.256 --> 10:44.924
So, if you see news reports
about a Roy Mitchell

10:44.959 --> 10:49.228
from three towns over who was
accused of all sorts of nonsense

10:49.264 --> 10:52.832
and left in a hurry,
it's got nothing to do with me,

10:52.867 --> 10:55.768
because, again, I'm...

10:55.804 --> 10:57.570
Herbert.

11:01.209 --> 11:04.343
Mom, Dad, Chris is gone!
He went to Woodstock.

11:04.379 --> 11:06.345
Now, Meg, nobody likes a snitch.

11:06.381 --> 11:08.781
I'm not a snitch,
I just tell it like it is.

11:08.817 --> 11:10.016
Damn it, Meg!

11:10.051 --> 11:12.351
Excuse me, Peter,
maybe I should handle this.

11:12.387 --> 11:14.353
- Mr. Brady?
- You know, Meg,

11:14.389 --> 11:16.989
when you tattle on someone,
you're not just telling on them,

11:17.025 --> 11:18.591
you're telling on yourself.

11:18.626 --> 11:21.427
And by tattling on someone,
you're really just telling them,

11:21.462 --> 11:22.995
"I'm a tattletale."

11:23.031 --> 11:25.531
Now, is that the tale you want to tell?

11:25.567 --> 11:28.568
- I never thought of it that way.
- Thanks, Mr. Brady.

11:28.603 --> 11:30.436
That's right. Mike Brady.

11:30.471 --> 11:34.040
So, if you see any news reports
about an actor named Robert Reed

11:34.075 --> 11:37.577
from three towns over who was
accused of all sorts of nonsense

11:37.612 --> 11:40.479
and left in a hurry,
it's got nothing to do with me,

11:40.515 --> 11:43.416
because, again, I'm...

11:43.451 --> 11:44.917
Mike Brady.

11:44.953 --> 11:46.452
Chris went to Woodstock?

11:46.487 --> 11:48.321
I got to get there and bring him back

11:48.356 --> 11:49.922
before something bad happens.

12:10.411 --> 12:13.946
Honey, I'm taking the compact;
save a little on gas.

12:28.096 --> 12:30.429
Cars are bigger back now.

12:38.806 --> 12:40.773
Oh, no, my dad!

12:40.808 --> 12:42.375
Chris!

12:42.410 --> 12:45.611
He'll be here in ten minutes.
I got to hide!

12:47.815 --> 12:50.950
- Chris!
- Dad, what are you doing here?

12:50.985 --> 12:54.086
- I'm here to bring you home.
- Well, I'm not going.

12:54.122 --> 12:56.489
I'm staying here and smoking dope.

12:56.524 --> 12:59.492
What? No son of mine
is gonna be smoking dope.

12:59.527 --> 13:03.562
Why not, Dad? Is it any worse
than your three martini lunch?

13:03.598 --> 13:05.231
What?! No son of mine

13:05.266 --> 13:07.199
is gonna be drinking
a three martini lunch.

13:07.235 --> 13:10.836
Why not, Dad? Is it any worse
than your five Scotch breakfast?

13:10.872 --> 13:12.538
What?! No son of mine

13:12.573 --> 13:14.674
is gonna be drinking
a five Scotch breakfast.

13:14.709 --> 13:16.609
Why not, Dad? Is it any worse than

13:16.644 --> 13:20.046
your "three X's drawn on
the jug" corn whiskey moonshine?

13:20.081 --> 13:23.616
What?! No son of mine
drink anything but rice wine.

13:23.651 --> 13:27.520
That's right, we trace
our early family roots to Asia.

13:27.555 --> 13:30.189
Listen, Chris,
your mother and I love you.

13:30.224 --> 13:32.858
We just want you to stay a kid
a little bit longer.

13:32.894 --> 13:36.462
- You understand?
- Um, y-yeah.

13:36.497 --> 13:38.965
I'm glad. 'Cause the thing is, Chris,

13:39.000 --> 13:41.734
drugs turn you into someone you're not.

13:41.769 --> 13:44.904
But you know what?
We love you just how you are.

13:44.939 --> 13:47.840
That's why I'm so grateful I got
here before you dropped acid

13:47.875 --> 13:49.675
or took any kind of drugs that...

13:49.711 --> 13:51.911
Y-You're trippin' right now, aren't you?

13:51.946 --> 13:54.513
- A little.
- All right.

13:54.549 --> 13:56.983
Hey, why is Pete Townshend
taking pictures of you?

13:57.018 --> 13:59.151
He's been doing that all day long.

13:59.187 --> 14:00.886
It's for a book.

14:00.922 --> 14:02.822
I don't think it's for a book.

14:06.861 --> 14:10.229
Okay, I'm off to do my paper route.

14:10.264 --> 14:11.964
Wow, Peter, your plan worked

14:12.000 --> 14:13.966
to show Chris how horrible drugs are.

14:14.002 --> 14:15.968
Quiet, Lois, I'm trying to watch TV.

14:16.004 --> 14:18.204
That's one small step...

14:18.239 --> 14:20.573
Ladies and gentlemen, the Beatles.

14:20.608 --> 14:22.475
The flash now official,

14:22.510 --> 14:24.810
President Kennedy has been killed.

14:24.846 --> 14:26.312
God, there's nothing on.

14:26.347 --> 14:28.581
♪ Just sit right back
and you'll hear a tale ♪

14:28.616 --> 14:30.683
- There we go.
- ♪ A tale of a fateful trip... ♪

15:05.956 --> 15:09.391
Lois, why is my son playing
with a vacuum like a girl?

15:09.426 --> 15:11.192
Here, play with this discus and javelin.

15:11.228 --> 15:13.161
Then you can grow up like a man,

15:13.196 --> 15:15.430
like track and field star Bruce Jenner,

15:15.465 --> 15:17.432
the greatest man in the world.

15:17.467 --> 15:19.668
Maybe you'll even end up
on a box of Wheaties.

15:19.703 --> 15:22.370
Didn't these used to have nuts in 'em?

15:23.740 --> 15:26.708
Chris, I see in the paper
that Vietnam is still going on,

15:26.743 --> 15:30.045
and as a blue collar TV dad,
I demand you enlist.

15:30.080 --> 15:31.880
Peter, you can't send Chris to Vietnam.

15:31.915 --> 15:33.348
They're bombing women and children.

15:33.383 --> 15:35.383
You have no idea
how bad it is over there.

15:35.419 --> 15:37.852
I don't? Oh, I know war, my friend.

15:37.888 --> 15:40.155
I stormed the beaches at Normandy.

15:41.992 --> 15:43.758
<i>Of course, it was 1958,</i>

15:43.794 --> 15:45.927
<i>so the resistance wasn't quite as stiff.</i>

15:45.962 --> 15:48.330
No running.

15:48.365 --> 15:49.664
I said no running!

15:49.700 --> 15:52.167
I'm not running!

15:52.202 --> 15:54.436
Chris, you are going to Vietnam
and doing your duty,

15:54.471 --> 15:55.770
like Quagmire did.

15:55.806 --> 15:57.739
As a matter of fact, his tour just ended

15:57.774 --> 15:59.474
and his transport
should be getting in soon.

15:59.509 --> 16:01.009
So let's head down to the airfield

16:01.044 --> 16:03.745
and give our soldiers
the respect they deserve.

16:08.018 --> 16:10.352
Welcome home, Quagmire!

16:10.387 --> 16:13.288
Oh, good to see you back safely, Glenn.

16:13.323 --> 16:15.623
- Murderer!
- Hey, show some respect.

16:15.659 --> 16:17.025
Sorry.

16:17.060 --> 16:19.027
Oh, I left my carry-on
back on the plane.

16:19.062 --> 16:20.362
All right, when he comes back,

16:20.397 --> 16:22.097
we got to make him feel welcome again.

16:27.637 --> 16:29.104
Boy, the Clam sure looks different.

16:29.139 --> 16:31.272
Yeah, just be careful with
your collars on the way in.

16:31.308 --> 16:32.607
They're kind of sharp.

17:04.775 --> 17:08.476
Yeah, we'll have a round
of beers for the table.

17:08.512 --> 17:09.978
Uhp, we got her, too.

17:10.013 --> 17:13.281
It's good to have you back from
the war in one piece, Quagmire.

17:13.316 --> 17:15.550
Yeah, some of us weren't so lucky.

17:15.585 --> 17:17.352
What are you talking about?
You never went to Vietnam.

17:17.387 --> 17:18.620
Hell I didn't!

17:18.655 --> 17:21.322
I went to the draft board
and told them I was gay,

17:21.358 --> 17:24.192
and then got hit by a bus
on the walk home.

17:27.898 --> 17:30.231
So, Quagmire, Chris is
shipping out tomorrow morning,

17:30.267 --> 17:32.033
and he's pretty nervous.

17:32.068 --> 17:35.103
I know you enjoy staring, like,
3,000 feet out into dead space,

17:35.138 --> 17:37.439
but you got any good stories
I could tell him

17:37.474 --> 17:39.107
about how cool 'Nam is?

17:39.142 --> 17:41.810
You know, there's lots of things
you expect in war...

17:41.845 --> 17:44.379
Carnage, the sleepless nights...

17:44.414 --> 17:46.514
But what they don't prepare you for

17:46.550 --> 17:49.050
is the incessant use of "Fortunate Son."

17:50.787 --> 17:53.087
<i>I'd hear that song
any time I was in a helicopter.</i>

17:55.058 --> 17:57.592
<i>Or taking a swift boat
deep into the jungle,</i>

17:57.627 --> 17:59.928
<i>that song again.</i>

17:59.963 --> 18:01.830
<i>My penis would even play "Fortunate Son"</i>

18:01.865 --> 18:03.539
<i>while I was visiting a whorehouse.</i>

18:03.540 --> 18:06.786
♪ It ain't me, it ain't me ♪

18:06.787 --> 18:10.235
♪ I ain't no millionaire's son, no... ♪

18:10.738 --> 18:12.592
Oh, Lord, that's horrible!

18:12.632 --> 18:14.798
There must have been
some other song you heard.

18:14.834 --> 18:16.467
Yeah, there was.

18:16.502 --> 18:19.103
That "There's something
happening here" song.

18:19.138 --> 18:20.538
Hey, I just realized,

18:20.573 --> 18:22.806
your clothes kind of
look like the jungle.

18:22.842 --> 18:24.708
Shut up, Joe! You weren't there!

18:24.744 --> 18:27.111
You don't know!
I'll kill you! I'll kill you!

18:27.146 --> 18:28.879
You didn't hear the songs I heard!

18:28.915 --> 18:30.981
Oh, my God,
Chris ships out in the morning!

18:31.017 --> 18:33.817
I got to get him out to there.
I thought this war would be fun,

18:33.853 --> 18:36.587
you know, just go over there
and fight the Donkey Kong.

18:36.622 --> 18:39.023
We were trying to fight the war our way,

18:39.058 --> 18:41.091
the way we'd always fought wars,

18:41.127 --> 18:44.628
but we were gradually climbing
steel beams and ladders,

18:44.664 --> 18:46.463
and the Donkey Kong knew that.

18:46.499 --> 18:50.601
It was barrel after barrel.
We lost a lot of good men.

18:50.636 --> 18:52.636
And so many quarters.

18:58.477 --> 19:00.978
Now, listen up! When you hear your name,

19:01.013 --> 19:03.213
I want you to sound off smartly
and move out.

19:03.249 --> 19:05.215
- Capo.
- Yes, sir.

19:05.251 --> 19:07.151
- Sumner.
- Yes, sir.

19:07.186 --> 19:08.385
Griffin.

19:10.656 --> 19:12.289
Chris Griffin!

19:12.325 --> 19:14.925
Yes, sir.

19:14.961 --> 19:17.461
I didn't think this through.

19:17.496 --> 19:19.463
Hey, where you going?!
What's the matter with you?!

19:19.498 --> 19:22.900
I said put your duffel bag right there!

19:24.437 --> 19:27.471
♪ Hey, guys, wait ♪

19:27.506 --> 19:29.406
♪ I'm not supposed to be here ♪

19:29.442 --> 19:31.642
♪ Stop, halt ♪

19:31.677 --> 19:33.577
♪ My name's not Chris, it's Peter ♪

19:33.613 --> 19:35.412
♪ He's late ♪

19:35.448 --> 19:37.081
♪ He's probably at the theater ♪

19:37.116 --> 19:41.485
♪ Watching a '70s movie ♪

19:41.520 --> 19:45.656
<i>♪ Maybe The French Connection ♪</i>

19:45.691 --> 19:49.860
<i>♪ Or possibly Godfather II ♪</i>

19:49.895 --> 19:54.298
♪ The number, not "also" ♪

19:54.333 --> 19:58.102
♪ Let my son die ♪

19:59.372 --> 20:02.172
♪ Let my son die ♪

20:02.208 --> 20:07.177
♪ My son, not me ♪

20:07.213 --> 20:10.180
♪ Let his son die ♪

20:11.217 --> 20:14.718
♪ Let his son die ♪

20:14.754 --> 20:18.188
♪ His son, not him ♪

20:18.224 --> 20:19.690
♪ Oh, whoa ♪

20:19.725 --> 20:22.192
- ♪ Let his son die ♪
- ♪ Let him die ♪

20:22.228 --> 20:23.661
♪ Locked in a cage ♪

20:23.696 --> 20:27.798
- ♪ Let his son die ♪
- ♪ Like John McCain ♪

20:27.833 --> 20:32.636
- ♪ His son, not him ♪
- ♪ Can't raise his arms. ♪

20:36.102 --> 20:38.102
Well, that's all we got tonight.
Hope you enjoyed it.

20:38.137 --> 20:41.365
And I hope the exhibits here at
The Museum of Television & Radio

20:41.366 --> 20:43.511
<i>don't come to life like
Night at the Museum.</i>

20:43.512 --> 20:46.473
- Hey, Peter!
- Don Knotts!

20:46.474 --> 20:48.635
- I just want to talk.
- Stay away from me!

20:48.636 --> 20:50.561
Andy Griffith used to beat me up.

20:50.562 --> 20:53.795
He was a big drinker.
Ask Ron Howard, he'll tell you.

20:53.796 --> 20:55.264
Help! Someone!

20:55.265 --> 20:58.367
Tim Conway was sleeping with
half the Apple Dumpling Gang.

20:58.368 --> 21:00.733
You two were the only ones
in the Apple Dumpling Gang!

21:00.734 --> 21:03.293
You're doing the math now, aren't you?

21:03.671 --> 21:06.671
Thank you for watching "Family Guy" on
www.FOX-fan.ru